---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { doc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['lanes', 'Lanes and the home screen'],
  ['branches', 'Branches and worktrees'],
  ['composer', 'Talk to the agent'],
  ['review', 'Review what it did'],
  ['models', 'Models, thinking, and context'],
  ['merge', 'Pull requests and merging'],
  ['slack', 'Slack thread links'],
  ['notifications', 'Notifications'],
  ['moves', 'Move a lane'],
  ['brain', 'Keep knowledge with Brain'],
  ['subagents', 'Split work across agents'],
  ['artifacts', 'Artifacts'],
  ['history', 'Resume, rewind, and fork'],
  ['routines', 'Routines'],
  ['skills', 'Skills'],
  ['settings', 'Settings worth knowing'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Daily work"
  eyebrow="Guide / using Phosphor"
  description="Everything after setup: run several lanes, steer the agent, review and merge its work, and use artifacts, routines, and skills. Each section links to its reference."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="lanes">
    <h2>Lanes and the home screen</h2>
    <p>
      Every chat is a lane: one agent, usually on its own branch and worktree. The sidebar lists
      lanes by project, with their status, branch, and a PR chip once there’s a pull request.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Home</strong> shows a local workbench with stable lane tiles, observed activity and bounded
        background-run previews. Its ledger shows usage and live processes. All machines home groups lanes
        into Needs you, Working and Recent, labelled with their machine and folder.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Find a lane</strong> with the magnifier in a project’s header. It matches title, branch,
        and PR number.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Right-click a lane</strong> to pin it, give it a marker, open its pull request, suspend
        it to free memory, fork or clone it, export it as HTML, copy its spend, or delete it. Double-click
        to rename. Drag rows to reorder them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('lanes.md')}>Lanes reference</a> · <a href={doc('ui-shell.md')}
        >Sidebar and window</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="branches">
    <h2>Branches and worktrees</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>new branch</strong> box under the home composer decides whether a chat gets its own
      branch and worktree. It’s on by default and branches off trunk. Turn it off for a quick question,
      or to keep working on the branch that’s already checked out.
    </p>
    <p>
      The branch control in the top bar searches branches, switches between the main checkout and
      worktrees, creates a worktree from a base you pick, pulls trunk, and updates a worktree from
      trunk when it falls behind. Deleting a lane that’s alone in its worktree offers to remove the
      worktree too.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('worktrees.md')}>Worktrees reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="composer">
    <h2>Talk to the agent</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>While it works:</strong> Enter queues a steering message, delivered after the current
        tool calls. Option+Enter (Alt+Enter) queues a follow-up for when it finishes. Esc stops the turn.
      </li><li><strong>@</strong> searches the workspace and adds a file reference.</li><li>
        <strong>Images:</strong> paste or drag them in. Click one to mark it up with boxes, arrows, and
        text before sending.
      </li><li>
        <strong>!command</strong> runs a shell command and adds its output to the context. <strong
          >!!command</strong
        > runs it without sending the output to the model.
      </li><li>
        <strong>/</strong> lists commands from pi, your prompt templates, and your extensions.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Rules · MCP</strong> next to the attach button turns CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md on or off
        for the lane, and enables or disables MCP servers.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Local Desktop drafts, attachments included, survive switching lanes and quitting. Browser
      drafts are not a durable offline archive. Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+X opens a taller field for long
      prompts.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('chat.md#composer')}>Composer reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="review">
    <h2>Review what it did</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The transcript:</strong> each tool call is a row you can expand for its arguments and
        output. Edits open as diffs. Cmd/Ctrl+F searches the whole session, tool output included.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Changes:</strong> observed tool changes with their diffs, not a forensic inventory of
        every external write. Local session-baseline Revert restores a file’s working copy; Host lanes
        do not offer that baseline revert.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Files:</strong> an explorer and an editor with tabs, search, and save.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Terminal:</strong> tabbed terminals for the lane’s folder.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Panes open from the top bar, one at a time beside the chat. Drag the divider, dock the pane
      left or right, or make it fullscreen. Each Desktop lane remembers its own layout. The browser
      has a read-only text file view, no Monaco editor or split panes. Workspace content search is
      local; filename browsing is a separate capability.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('files.md')}>Files and editor</a> · <a href={doc('terminal.md')}>Terminal</a> · <a
        href={doc('chat.md#tool-renderers')}>Tool cards</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="models">
    <h2>Models, thinking, and context</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Model menu:</strong> every model you’re signed into, searchable, with stars and recents.
        Switch mid-session and the conversation carries over.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Thinking:</strong> pick the level for the next turn, from off to max, as far as the model
        supports.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Context meter:</strong> how full the context is, tokens and cost, an estimate of what’s
        filling it, and your Claude or ChatGPT plan usage.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Compaction:</strong> sessions compact at a shared context budget, 200k tokens by default.
        Change it in Settings → Agent, or compact now from the lane’s ⋮ menu.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Running low on a plan? <a href="/guides/accounts/#pivot">Move the lane to another one</a>. <a
        href={doc('chat.md#what-the-context-meters-popover-shows')}>Context meter reference</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="merge">
    <h2>Pull requests and merging</h2>
    <p>
      The lane’s PR pill opens an inspector with Overview, Checks, Reviews, Threads and Activity. It
      distinguishes owned PRs from ones being reviewed or merely mentioned. Track or Hide writes
      lane metadata only for an open local lane; those actions are not available on Host lanes.
      Current Hosts can still supply PR status and inspector detail.
    </p>
    <Capture
      image={shot('lane-pr')}
      alt="PR inspector with fictional checks, review status and a hand-off action."
      caption="Read the checks and the review, not just a green colour"
    />
    <p>
      Phosphor stays read-only on GitHub through the gh CLI. Fix, Nudge, Address and Update draft a
      message into the composer; nothing is sent until you send it. A PR with no checks is not green
      CI. Stale or unavailable results stay labelled; confirm uncertain merge readiness on GitHub.
    </p>
    <p>
      A worktree lane’s agent is instructed to commit and open a PR when done, but the inspector
      does not push, create or merge one. The separate local Git Merge flow remains an explicit
      action and walks you through dirty worktrees first. It is not an automatic GitHub merge.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('lanes.md#pr-status')}>PR status reference</a> · <a href="/watch/#lane-to-review"
        >Watch the review workflow</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="slack">
    <h2>Return to a Slack thread</h2>
    <p>
      The Slack pill appears when user prompts or settled assistant text mention valid Slack thread
      links. It is not an inbox, a Slack connection indicator or a background reader. Search tool
      output and a still-streaming reply do not add threads.
    </p>
    <Capture
      image={shot('slack-pill')}
      alt="Slack pill listing fictional thread links already mentioned in the lane."
      caption="Links from the conversation, not a synced inbox"
    />
    <p>
      Open a thread, copy its link, or append it to your unsent draft without replacing what you
      typed. Using Slack tools is separate: configure the <a href="/guides/connectors/#slack"
        >MCP connector</a
      > and authorize its scopes.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="notifications">
    <h2>Come back to what needs you</h2>
    <p>
      The top-bar bell opens the notification drawer. It retains lane notices, actions, warnings and
      errors after their toast leaves. New and Earlier separate unseen from seen; entries are
      memory-only, not a durable activity archive.
    </p>
    <Capture
      image={shot('notifications')}
      alt="Notification drawer showing fictional lane and PR notices with return actions."
      caption="Return to the lane, with the reason still attached"
    />
    <p>
      Important PR changes for owned This Mac lanes can produce a toast and drawer entry, plus an OS
      notification while Phosphor is not focused. The first read after launch is only a baseline;
      failed reads are not change events. PR hand-offs still draft rather than send. Turn off PR
      status in Settings → Lanes to silence those notices.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('ui-shell.md#global-surfaces')}>Notification behavior and retention</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="moves">
    <h2>Continue on another machine</h2>
    <p>
      Move to… makes a checked snapshot copy of an idle lane to another workspace or connected Host,
      from Desktop or, between Hosts, from the browser. The original remains and the new lane waits
      for you to open it. Commit dirty Git work first; credentials, processes and routine schedules
      do not move. This is separate from switching a provider account.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/guides/machines/#moves">Move prerequisites, contents and recovery</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="brain">
    <h2>Keep the decision behind the change</h2>
    <p>
      Enable Brain on Desktop and queue a selected lane. Its durable memories and relationships
      save when the read finishes, with Undo. Future local lanes can recall them. Local embeddings
      do not make extraction offline: the lane’s provider reads scrubbed conversation text.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/guides/brain/">Set up Brain and follow its knowledge</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="subagents">
    <h2>Split work across agents</h2>
    <p>
      With the <a href="/guides/extensions/#subagents">Subagents package</a> installed, ask the agent
      to split independent work, or enable Swarm for standing authorization to delegate when useful. The
      current-work summary distinguishes parent activity from reported child activity. Open the dockable
      Subagents pane for tasks, bounded activity and results. Missing status is unavailable, not proof
      that work finished. Turning Swarm off does not stop existing children.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/guides/swarm/">Multi-agent workflow and Swarm guide</a> · <a
        href={doc('chat.md#sub-agents')}>Sub-agents reference</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="artifacts">
    <h2>Artifacts</h2>
    <p>
      Ask for a plan, a doc, a diagram, a chart, or an HTML page as an artifact. It opens in the
      Artifacts pane next to the chat and keeps every version, even after the session compacts. The
      Artifacts page in the sidebar lists artifacts by machine, including versions that outlive the
      lane. Copy reference can point another same-machine lane at an artifact. Desktop HTML previews
      run isolated with no network access; the PWA offers static alternatives or source, not
      scripted HTML execution.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('extensions.md#the-artifact-tools-and-what-each-one-costs')}
        >Artifacts reference</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="history">
    <h2>Resume, rewind, and fork</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Old sessions reopen from the sidebar with their full history.</li><li>
        <strong>Rewind to here</strong> on one of your messages, or Esc Esc for a picker, branches the
        chat from just before it and puts your text back in the composer. The old version folds away.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Fork</strong> and <strong>Clone</strong> in a lane’s menu make a copy and keep both.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={doc('lanes.md#forks-and-saved-history')}>Forks and saved history</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="routines">
    <h2>Routines</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Routines</strong> in the sidebar runs a task on a schedule: hourly, daily, weekly, monthly,
      once, or a cron expression. Each run starts a fresh lane with the model you picked. Routines run
      on their execution machine while it’s awake, not in the cloud. A Host owns its own routines; create
      them paused and explicitly enable unattended full tool access. <strong>Make routine…</strong> in
      a lane’s menu drafts one from that lane.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('routines.md')}>Routines reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="skills">
    <h2>Skills</h2>
    <p>
      A skill is a written procedure the agent can follow. The Skills page in the sidebar lists your
      own and the ones your packages bring. Create, import, or export your own there, or add a
      reviewed one from discovery. Skills can be shared between machines; that does not copy
      credentials or configuration.
    </p>
    <p><a href={doc('ui-shell.md#global-surfaces')}>Skills reference</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="settings">
    <h2>Settings worth knowing</h2>
    <p>
      Search Settings by name. Device settings are separate from Configure → machine settings;
      Folders replaces the old Workspaces label. Brain, local Voice and writing assistance are
      Desktop capabilities, not browser features. A dictated prompt still reaches your provider when
      sent.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Keyboard:</strong> Cmd/Ctrl+K opens the command palette, Cmd/Ctrl+N starts a session,
        and Cmd/Ctrl+B toggles the sidebar. Settings → Keybindings lists the rest.
      </li><li><strong>Appearance:</strong> light, dark, or follow the system.</li><li>
        <strong>Agent → Directives:</strong> what Phosphor adds to every lane’s system prompt, shown in
        full before it’s sent.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Optimization:</strong> Headroom, an optional local proxy that restructures large JSON
        tool results before they enter the context. Its Advisor reads your session files and settings
        and points out what’s worth changing.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Advanced → Maintenance:</strong> finds worktrees of lanes that already landed and reports
        how much space they hold. It deletes them only if you ask.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Send feedback</strong> in the Settings footer opens a prefilled GitHub issue with your
        rating and notes. Nothing about your code or sessions is attached.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={doc('settings.md')}>Settings reference</a></p>
  </section>
</Guide>
